-Twenty-four objects in six rows across a wide room, and eighteen of them are things this museum has never shown.
+Twenty-four objects in six rows. A familiar shape has company: beside it stand other versions of what it could have been. Two wait on each side of the aisle. To see a whole family, you have to cross the floor.
 
-Every artifact in the boolean chapter is a family. Each one declares an axis — a named dial with four settings — and every placement anywhere else in the building shows you exactly one of those settings, the one it shipped with. This hall is the only place the other three stand up. One artifact per band, its four values laid across the room, two on your left and two on your right with the walking lane between them, so you meet the family two at a time and have to cross the floor to finish the sentence.
+Each row changes a named setting within one artifact. Some settings alter the shape. Others alter how much of its making becomes visible. These are authored possibilities: somebody chose the values, gave them names and decided which one would meet us first.
 
-The six you have already met are here too, one per band, and in five of the six they are the first thing you come to on the left. That is a convention rather than an accident: the registry lists a family's values with the default first, and this hall was laid out from the registry. So each row starts with the object as you know it and then shows you what else it was willing to be.
+In the previous room, a short list of operations opened into many possible bodies. Here the museum has made another selection from those possibilities. Its arrangement can be investigated too.
 
-## A variant is a claim somebody published
-
-The four settings are not in the object. They are in a file. Each artifact's code holds a list of the values it will accept, and the registry — `commons/artifacts/registry/boolean_surfaces.json` and its neighbours — holds a second list, which is what every tool in this project reads: the catalogue builder, the sweep, the deck, the audit. The object obeys the first list. The museum, the gallery pages and this room are built from the second.
-
-So a band in this hall is a published claim about an object, standing next to the object. That is a rare arrangement, and it makes the room usable for something other than looking: you can check.
-
-Three of the six bands here disagree with their own code about what order the ladder goes in. Two agree exactly. One agrees and then checks itself at runtime, which no other artifact in the chapter does.
-
-## The band that agrees
+## How much of a cut do you want to see?
 
 <!-- @csg_difference_demo -->
 
+The first row begins with a box that has a bite taken out of it. Its surface gives no account of the sphere that made the hollow. In the next version, a pale volume occupies the missing region. Something you could look through has become something you can also look at.
+
+Further along, transparent operands let you see the original box and sphere together. At the last version, the expression names their relation. The carved result remains; its surroundings have begun to explain it.
+
+The source gives these four readings a vocabulary:
+
 ```gdscript
-# csg_difference_demo.gd:73
 const WORKINGS: PackedStringArray = ["outcome", "trace", "operands", "expression"]
 ```
 
-Four readings of one cut. At `outcome` you get the answer and nothing else, a box with a bite out of it. At `trace` the removed material comes back as a ghost, occupying the space it was taken from. At `operands` both original solids return, transparent, so you can see the box and the sphere that were consumed. At `expression` a labelled rod names the operation out loud.
+More disclosure need not mean a better encounter every time. The ghost can make a cut legible while making the hollow harder to see. The bare result may let a form surprise us before we know its recipe. Cross back to the first object with what the others have shown you. Its silence has changed.
 
-The registry declares those four words in that order, and so does the code, and so does the second axis. This is the only band in the room where the published claim and the object match on both dials, and it is worth standing in front of because it shows what the ladder is *for*: it is not four decorations, it is one operation shown at four degrees of disclosure. The object gets progressively less confident about its own result as you walk right.
-
-There is one seam even here, and it is small and revealing. The dial that positions the cut names only two of its four settings in the code that reads it; `centred` — the one the artifact advertises as producing a clean internal void — arrives through the wildcard. It is a defined fallback rather than a separate named branch. The same path also receives an unrecognised value, so reading the word alone does not tell us whether that value was deliberately selected.
-
-## The ladder, published out of order
+## A different way through the row
 
 <!-- @csg_union_demo -->
 
+In the union row, the first pair already overlaps. Beside it, a box and sphere stand apart. The next versions bring them close and then deeply into one another. The route across the gallery is not the order in which one might teach their approach.
+
+That order comes from a catalogue convention: show the familiar default first. In this family the default is called `lobed`. The code also names a progression:
+
 ```gdscript
-# csg_union_demo.gd:119 — the code's ladder
 const FUSIONS: PackedStringArray = ["distinct", "necked", "lobed", "single"]
 ```
 
-In the code those four are monotonic. They are four multiples of one offset, running down: far apart, nearly touching, overlapping, concentric. Walk them in that order and you watch two things become one, in four steps, with the topology changing under you.
+Find the separated pair, then the narrow connection, then the broader overlap and the concentric arrangement. Your walk now follows decreasing separation, even though the objects have stayed where they were. A change in your route has made another argument from the same row.
 
-Walk this band left to right and you get `lobed, distinct, necked, single` — the registry's order, which is the order this hall was built from, and which starts you two-thirds of the way up the ladder, sends you back to the bottom, then part way up again, then to the top. The sequence is the same four states. The gradual progression is interrupted. The catalogue asks us to recognise the familiar object first; the tutorial asks us to follow the changing offset. Those are two different orders for the same four objects. Start at `distinct`, then find `necked`, `lobed` and `single`. What became legible when you changed your route?
+The separated pair still counts as a union. Togetherness here does not require a continuous skin. At the other end, one primitive can hide within the region retained by the other. A single-looking body need not tell us how many operands took part in making it.
 
-The registry did not do this carelessly; the entry says the order is the sweep's tile order, default leftmost. But the disagreement has consequences past this room: a third file, `fusion_ladder.json`, declares the ladder in the code's order and states that it took that order character for character from `csg_union_demo.gd:128`. So one artifact cites this object's code as canonical while the object's own registry entry publishes something else, and both are read by tools.
+A default becomes powerful when it is the only version we ever meet. This row gives it neighbours. The familiar form loses some of its authority to say what the artifact is.
 
-## A rung that is only a name
+## The names of agreement
 
 <!-- @csg_intersection_demo -->
 
-```gdscript
-# csg_intersection_demo.gd:234 — three of the four are here
-match consensus:
-    "unanimous": ...
-    "narrow": ...
-    "token": ...
-    _: ...
-```
+The intersection row keeps only shared space. Compare its broad survivor with the small remainder further along. The names offered for these settings include `unanimous`, `broad`, `narrow` and `token`.
 
-The same displacement, one band down. `consensus` asks how much has to be shared before anything survives, and the four values run from complete agreement to a token overlap.
+Those words bring a social scene into a geometric one. But the objects have not negotiated. Moving the same operands changes the region they share. An intersection cannot contain more than either operand; it can equal one of them if that one is contained in the other.
 
-`broad` reaches its position through the wildcard branch, which returns the shipped offset. It therefore has a defined result even without a branch bearing its name. But the fallback also accepts other unmatched inputs. A familiar result can conceal an unfamiliar instruction: to audit the encounter, we need both the selected value and what actually ran.
+The current catalogue puts `broad` first. It is the ordinary answer from which the others appear to depart. Standing several versions together makes that choice visible. The beginning of a row has quietly suggested a centre.
 
-And it is the one the registry puts first, so this band opens on it.
+Stay with the small survivor. Does calling it a token overlap help you see it, or tell you too quickly how much it should matter? The amount shared and the value of sharing are different questions.
 
-## Four degrees of removal, read from somewhere else
+## How much can go?
 
 <!-- @subtraction_suite -->
 
-```gdscript
-# subtraction_suite.gd:162
-## The vocabulary and the ratios, read from the family rather than retyped.
-var _breach_table: Dictionary = FONTANA_SOURCE.BREACH
-```
+Here the cutter grows in relation to the body. The settings are called `pierced`, `opened`, `severed` and `husk`. As the sphere reaches through more of the box, a hollow opens to the outside and the remaining pieces change their relation.
 
-Here is the counter-example, and the hall needs one. `breach` asks how far a removal can go before the thing is gone, and its four rungs — pierced, opened, severed, husk — are not four numbers in this file. They are read out of `fontana_puncture.gd`, another artifact entirely, which holds them as ratios of the body's own size: 0.54, 0.68, 0.74, 0.80. The enum, the table and the registry agree on all four words, in the same order.
+Each setting repeats the cut in several kinds of display. A painted object stands on a plinth. Another appears beside its transparent operands. A tray gathers specimens. The same operation moves between sculpture, explanation and collection; each arrangement gives your eye a different task.
 
-The bench makes an argument out of that: because the ratios are read rather than retyped, they cannot drift. Which is true of every one of them except the one the file retypes fifty lines later as a literal `0.68`, inside the function whose whole purpose is the anti-drift claim. One number, in the file that exists to say numbers do not need to be repeated.
+The cutter's radius is a fraction of the box's size. The sequence borrows those fractions from Fontana Puncture, an earlier work in this museum. A borrowed rule can carry a relation onward while the new surroundings change what we ask of it.
 
-## The degenerate case, painted
+There need not be a preferred amount of removal. A narrow opening might hold your attention; a wider one might admit a body; another cut might undo the enclosure you wanted. The name of the setting cannot choose that purpose for you.
+
+## A picture that holds the failure still
 
 <!-- @coincident_face -->
 
+Amber and blue alternate in a thin band where two drawn faces overlap. The exhibit speaks about surfaces competing for the same depth, the flicker a renderer can produce when it cannot reliably separate them.
+
+This band holds the competition still. Its colours are assigned by the index of each bar:
+
 ```gdscript
-# coincident_face.gd:257 — the coin toss
 col = color_a if (i % 2 == 0) else color_b
 ```
 
-This band is about what happens when two faces occupy the same plane and the depth test has no answer — the flicker every 3D artist has seen, where a surface tears into stripes of two materials because finite depth precision can leave the two surfaces competing for the same depth value.
+Even, odd, amber, blue. We met that economy in the arrays. Here it draws an explanation of uncertainty using a completely decided pattern.
 
-The striped diagram contains no Boolean operation and does not rely on z-fighting. There is not one CSG node in the file; the four places the string "CSG" appears are all text on labels. The stripes are the line above: even bars one colour, odd bars the other, alternating by index. And they are drawn at an explicitly unambiguous depth, four millimetres in front of everything else, so the renderer is never asked the question the artifact is named after. It is a diagram of a failure, executed by a mechanism that cannot fail.
+Across the four copies, the faces separate, nearly meet, overlap or give blue a small lead in depth. The diagram makes the alternatives easy to compare. Its striped band is not a live measure of which surface the renderer favours at your current viewpoint. A picture of a failure and an instance of that failure do different work. We can want the clarity of one without mistaking it for the other.
 
-That is not a cheat, exactly — a diagram can hold still a failure whose appearance depends on the camera and rendering setup. But it is worth knowing what you are looking at, because there *is* a real coincident face in this file and it is not the one on the wall: at the `coincident` setting the two rectangle fills are placed at the same depth, the same thickness, overlapping in x, and they genuinely contest. The artifact produced the thing it is about by accident, behind the drawing of it, and says nothing about it.
-
-The band's other seam is the same one union has: code and registry disagree on order, this time by moving the failure state to the front. The registry declares it in its own note, so it is a known disagreement rather than a hidden one.
